cture to assist the l brynn  researchers  and um but the major you know there's a  list of  a number of different software that we  keep um  available on these resources but as you  all know the major  uh impediment to doing bioinformatics  research is not  necessarily the hardware in the software  it's engaging with the expertise people  who know how to use  these tools and to put them together to  do meaningful  uh computational biology and  bioinformatics  so that brings me to the educational  resources and the lbrn hosts a variety  of training sessions  on hpc utilization scripting and  software utilization  and there's personnel available to  assist our researchers with use of those  hpc resources  if they want to access them directly and  they can contact  our associate director nyong kim for  details on those programs but they're  also available on the elderin website  and we really like you know we want to  expose the researchers  throughout our network to bioinformatics  methods  and this is done through the elderly  annual meeting which we're holding right  now  the summer research program and also our  annual conference on bioinformatics and  computational biology  so uh this last year  in early april was supposed to be our  eighth annual  bioinformatics conference but  unfortunately due to the timing and the  quick emergence of the pandemic  we had we didn't have time to pivot our  program  to a virtual format at that time so we  actually had to cancel the meeting  and as ramesh mentioned earlier we  already had uh lined up  a presentation a whole session from the  nih strides initiative which was going  to be led by todd reilly  and we had speak keynote speakers from  the amazon web services and google cloud  research  communities and and so we've reinvited  them this year  and this is just a you know promotion to  save the date  our uh annual conference is going to be  the april 15th through 17th so that's a  thursday friday saturday and we've  confirmed that the  nih stride session is going to be held  on that thursday april 15th  and then we'll also as we circulate  we'll be circulating the flyer  in the coming month about our program  and the other imminent bioinformatics  and computational biology speakers that  we're going to  have give keynote talks at that  conference  so in in terms of educating the  researchers  about bioinformatics the the landscape  has really  changed over the course the last decade  or so and and bioinformatics is  really not no longer a niche area that  can be served  by a handful of specialized researchers  who are you know computer scientists and  mathematicians  and nearly every discipline within  biomedical research now  has the need for bioinformatics methods  so we  really you know attempt to bridge this  gap between the biological researchers  and the computing researchers  and the challenge here though is that  you need to trade this  larger cohort of biomedical researchers  and the problem with that is that those  researchers  they need not be bioinformatics  specialists but some of them will have  very diverse backgrounds and this leads  to challenges with the traditional  format  of educational programs and i found this  when i was trying to give  some of the early lecture series on on  bioinformatics where i got very  disparate  reviews from the course and some  students said it went way too fast for  them it didn't have enough  details and other students said they  were bored to tears  you know so those were the ends of the  spectrum whereas you know the primary  bell-shaped curve where people who are  very satisfied with pace the course  but with the just the diverse  backgrounds that you have coming in some  people have been programming for a long  time  other people who haven't programmed at  all it's become very difficult to  you know hold a traditional course so  the emerging solution that we have  is a bit more reliance on technology to  provide  online learning opportunities that allow  our students  to really move at their own pace which  they really can't do in a traditional  classroom environment  but online they can do they can repeat  sessions they can go at their own pace  and so  um early on i had conducted some lecture  series and  and i worked with our media site team to  capture these lectures  and these lectures persist as two  full-fledged courses one which was an  introduction to informatics and the  other which was an  introduction to analysis of the  microbiota  and all of our researchers can take  advantage of this that  all the lectures and the slide sets are  recorded along with  um you know some homeworks and  assignments  and they're available at those above  urls  and i you know after posting this i  actually got uh  you know an email from some folks in the  northern new england clinical and  translational research network and they  they had found this material and they  asked me if it was okay for them to post  it  so that their investigators could  benefit from access to those resources  so this was a  sort of a broader impact that i hadn't  even anticipated until this email came  in  and i was being asked for permission for  other people to be able to use these  these recorded lecture series which  primarily i provided for the lbrm  and so in terms of our pui usage when we  did these lecture series we we  co-enrolled uh from some students from  lsu shreveport  which uh erska helped to to direct that  course  and also southern university in baton  rouge where wesley gray  helped to enroll students from there and  they were able to  to remotely view the lectures in the in  the material in  lectures was then supplemented with  on-site experiences  from the home instructors but this  process was really hard to scale up  due to the you know just the need for  us to be split across research and  teaching and all the things that we have  to do  and so more recently we've partnered the  alburn has established a partnership  with pine biotech and they've developed  a library of courses  that much like the lecture series the  students can take at their own pace  and this is going to address parts of  the critical need for education in this  field  and initially we we established this in  summer of 2018  and we got 25 educational licenses that  we purchased through the lbrn for use  by the lbr and pui campuses and then  additional licenses  were supported through the cct and all  of our attendees worked through hands-on  educational material that was provided  at pine biotech through their edu t bio  info course portal and this was  genomics metagenomics and and several  transcriptomics courses  and all of our students were then  encouraged to present project posters  at the elder and annual meeting which  many of them did  and because of the pilot's success it's  been since expanded into a partnership  and and i'm going to hand things over  right now to  elio brodsky because he really is  going to be better to give you an  overview of the edu both the educational  offerings that pine biotech has through  this partnership  in addition to some of the  bioinformatics pipelines that they can  support  thank you  so i wanted to um thank you chris for  the introduction  and i wanted to kind of take the time  to briefly introduce a few resources  that our team has developed to support  training and research in bioinformatics  as well as now data science for both  students and faculty to leverage  in their biomedical research or learn  about the publicly available data sets  and become proficient  in analytical methods to process analyze  and interpret these  effectively so the omics logic resources  are designed around  kiomics data types as chris mentioned  with big data cloud-based data analysis  tools  coding exercises in r and python as well  as  project-based tutorials that combine  biological or biomedical topics with  data analytics  statistical analysis principles and  curated data sets in a variety  of applications and so what our company  really does is provides an opportunity  for recent graduates and students to  leverage their skills  in bioinformatics and computational  biology to  train and mentor others with a growing  community of over 7 000  students worldwide and now a growing  community in louisiana which we're very  excited about  and so the academic oversight for work  is ensured by a close collaboration with  the tower bioinformatics research center  which is  at university of haifa in israel and is  directed by  dr alfred tauber and dr lena brodsky so  the center recently signed a memorandum  of understanding with louisiana state  university  to collaborate on research projects  expand these training programs and  establish faculty and student exchange  programs  so hopefully this will be possible after  the pandemic  but also due to the increased online  opportunities that have been highlighted  due to these unfortunate events as we  can see here in this virtual conference  hopefully virtual use of these resources  will be enhanced  so let me introduce the specific  solutions that we have been focused on  for the past several years recently as  was mentioned by chris  lbrn has started offering  research licenses to the tbi info  platform  which provides a simple and intuitive  user interface  for processing analysis and machine  learning methods for integration of  multi-onyx data  and to support the growing number of  users we're now offering processing the  data  on our shared server infrastructure as  well as on google  cloud so now we can run pipelines on our  servers  or you can send them to google cloud  directly and so we're now developing  pipelines that support the analysis of  omix data generated by the current  pandemic  such as the virology specific tools for  genomic data  generated by throughout this pandemic  and that includes evolutionary studies  host pathogen interaction and  interest host variation studies  importantly we're now focused on  developing user-friendly tools for  annotation and visualization of results  as well  and we hope that this will help link  significant signatures  found in genomic data with biologically  relevant visualization  and reporting capabilities and so we  hope that  these tools will help streamline the  analysis and reporting on important  genomic signatures  that will make the analysis results  clear to both as well as biologists  using these analytical pipelines we're  also  in final stages of completion for  validation of specific areas  of analysis that combine tools for  cancer research aging and virology  as well as a collection of tools for  multinomics integration which has been a  topic that has been discussed widely in  scientific literature  so these are uh available uh through the  lbrn pipelines  sorry licenses that could be requested  from the lbrm  we also realized that many users lack  some basic bioinformatics terminology  and necessary  kind of concepts to perform reproducible  analysis so in 2020  we launched a number of data science  training tutorials  combining our six omics specific course  collections  with this new resources developed to  help understand the code  behind many of the algorithms offering  training in statistical analysis  machine learning experimental design  data mining  and systems biology so these include  basic as well as more advanced tutorials  with browser-based  r and python exercises where users can  learn about scripting  some popular visualization data analysis  packages for  data science and learn how to apply  those to a variety of different data  types  so and as a result we started developing  a series of data science specific course  collections  that also break down the analytical and  different machine learning methods  into very short tutorials for  asynchronous use  and so we hope to increase using these  resources the capacity for data mining  and machine learning methods  so that those could be used effectively  to analyze  big data sets and do predictive analysis  and together with lbrn we're going to  start offering  these courses as program resources  they will be offered eventually with  lbrn certification  and we're now working with a number of  faculty  throughout the lprn campuses to help  integrate these asynchronous courses  into curricula offered throughout the  different puis  so to kind of briefly share with you  how we have used these materials over  the past year  and what are some of the ways that the  lbrm community can gain  access to these resources and materials  so as a part of our collaboration with  lbrn  we have this online complete  asynchronous course  that can be accessed by students  throughout the year at any time  starting with an introduction to  bioinformatics and learning about the  logic of data analysis  in application to various omics data  types so this is available  and i think we recently made an  announcement about that  so we will soon announce how people can  actually start that process  these basic materials are offered as an  introduction to bioinformatics module  which can be accessible to life science  students without  any previous experience in  bioinformatics the module covers the  basics of genomics in biomedical  applications  and we recently started the process of  offering this course as an undergraduate  course through the department of  biological sciences at lsu  and it can also be made available for  other campuses that might want to use it  as a gentle introduction to  bioinformatics  the coursework itself was developed  internally primarily but also  other contributors included faculty  such as this course on biomedical data  analysis developed by dr himanshu rajei  who graduated from lsu and is now a  professor at nicholls state university  in his course he offers an example of  pharma  pharmacogenomics testing using python  and gives a basic idea of what other  types of various biological data might  require  people to be familiar with  bioinformatics terminology  so based on the course work that was  already presented by chris  we also developed a course on  metagenomics to kind of  break it down into smaller pieces of  information that could be digested more  easily  and so these courses offer a variety of  processing tools that we've implemented  into the same  user-friendly interface and that  includes whole genome  mapping onto the database uh kind to  and data amplicon uh pipelines  with uh both a user-friendly and also  the coding aspect of visualization of  that data  so here you can see some of the examples  of the tutorials  including the uh the famous phylacy  package that is often used  we also have now a graduate course that  we offer jointly  with the division of biotechnology and  molecular medicine at louisiana state  university  it was led and organized by dr gus  kasulis  dr ramesh subramanian and the support of  dr farhana musarat  who is a postdoc at dr kusulus's lab  and as a result of that experience we  also formed a  graduate program on bioinformatics for  infectious diseases  where we use curated data sets from a  number of different epidemic outbreaks  to illustrate analysis methods relevant  for infectious disease monitoring  and biomedical research  using these resources we were able to  offer training to over 200 students in  the past year alone  throughout louisiana and so we collected  anonymous and specific feedback from  students and faculty  to help guide future development of  these resources  we learned that the materials resonated  well with the users and addressed a  pressing need among the student as well  as faculty  we also learned that the platform  developed a unique opportunity for many  to use  big data analysis tools for the first  time which otherwise they would not have  an opportunity to use  and many users reported a measurable  increase  in their understanding of technical  bioinformatics terminology  and understanding of how to practically  apply such methods  to different types of data sets and  challenges  and we're also excited to see many  undergraduate as well as  graduate students provide feedback for  the asynchronous training materials  the analytical pipelines that they've  used and participation in webinars that  was offered  in collaboration with lbrn and i think  that this is especially relevant because  we do have such a diverse population  of students that have very different  backgrounds and very different levels of  exposure to research  using bioinformatics and data science  and this includes both undergraduate  students and graduate students  throughout the elbow and primarily  undergraduate institutes  institutions as well as faculty that  were able to  learn about different aspects of  bioinformatics and even use  some of those tools in their own  research  so that's all i have for today thank you  for  inviting me to share this  all right uh thanks elia  um if there are any questions for any  either of the  either chris or elia now's a good time  um  the floor is open for questions if you  have any in there otherwise we'll  transition to dr  lee from there the only comment that we  make ramesh is that uh  all new funded projects will go through  review  by both course and for by our  statistician  before they are awarded uh and there  will be a  session where we're going to be  reviewing um  whether the appropriate tools have been  used or what else can be done to  help them move forward as we move  forward  and of course the same issue would be  with new applications coming in  they will be reviewed by both cores and  uh especially on the statistical side  to make sure that uh ahead of the game  we have been there  uh i think all of these things will be  available information on the  lbrn website um and  we you know i think we will be launching  a lot of these  ultimately would like to create a  curriculi in pui campuses that use those  tools  but we'll move forward with brn  certification  uh and hoping to have pui's adopt  certain of this material as chris uh  mentioned  in their own curricula as we move  forward so in the interest of time i  think we can proceed thank you
 

